<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lingqu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 214 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Lingqu Canal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lingqu is a canal in Xing'an county, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, completed around 214 BCE on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> to supply his armies campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a>. About 36 kilometres long, it links the Xiang River, flowing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, with the Li River, flowing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a>, and is among the oldest contour canals in the world. It remained in use for over two thousand years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lingqu.jpg" alt="Lingqu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Farm · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lingqu_Canal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
